description:
Plan a code cave detour (lesson 4.03): find caves, decide how many bytes to steal, and build the cave's code.

```cpp
struct Cave { std::size_t offset; std::size_t length; };

std::vector<Cave> findCaves(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& code, std::size_t minLength);
std::optional<std::size_t> bytesToSteal(const std::vector<std::size_t>& instructionLengths, std::size_t needed);
std::optional<std::vector<std::uint8_t>> buildCave(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& stolen, const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& newCode,
                                                    std::uint64_t caveAddress, std::uint64_t returnAddress);
```

- **findCaves** lists runs of the same filler byte, `0x00` or `0xCC`, at least `minLength` long, in order. A run is one byte value only (a run of `CC`s next to a run of `00`s is two caves).
- **bytesToSteal** takes the lengths of the instructions at the patch point, in order, and returns the total length of the fewest whole instructions that add up to at least `needed` bytes, or `std::nullopt` if they never do.
- **buildCave** returns the bytes placed at `caveAddress`: `newCode`, then the stolen instructions unchanged, then an `E9` jump back to `returnAddress`. If the stolen bytes start a relative branch or call (`E8`, `E9`, `EB`, `70`-`7F` or `0F 8x` as the first byte of the stolen bytes) it returns `std::nullopt`, because copying them unchanged would break them; so does a jump back that does not fit in 32 bits.

